  "sourceType": "SYNTHESISED",
  "sourceNotes": "Maple syrup tarts are part of the Canadian baking tradition (tarte au sirop d'érable), appearing in Quebec recipes from at least the 19th century. The combination with walnuts rather than pecans is a practical substitution that also produces a more bitter, adult flavour profile. Walnut and maple as a pairing appears in Canadian and American recipes, as well as in British baking from the 2000s onward when maple syrup became a mainstream store-cupboard ingredient in the UK. The method here is closer to pecan pie than to the pure maple custard version used in Quebec tradition.",
